Well, I'll summarize.
1) Putty settings are always the same since two years or more.

2) I used and use the Belkin-like cable (but it's anonymous) for 
Sun, SGI, HP, IBM, Apollo machines. The cable can be connected to a 
USB2Serial adapter on the notebook or a standard RS-232 on the main PC.

3) For Digital machines I always used a MMJ DECconnect cable with a MMJ-DB9 or
MMJ-DB25 adapter, marked Digital.

4) Since the DECserver hadn't any MMJ port I used the Belkin-like cable.
I noticed that I visualized all messages, but my keystrokes were lost.

5) So I resumed from the box a DECConnect MMJ cable and put a MMJ-DB9
and a MMJ-DB25 at the ends, since the DECServer has a DB25 port and the
USB2Serial or the standard RS-232 are DB9. Everything worked, without changing
*anything* else.

6) The same thing happened on a DEC2000 AXP. It'the first time I use the 
Belkin-like cable on a Digital machine. I always used cables and adapters
branded Digital.

7) I guess that Digital RS-232 management differs from the other brands ;)



