> In article <k5minm$iqb$1@tdi.cu.mi.it>, supervinx  <nessuno@libero.it> wrote:
>>
>> 7) I guess that Digital RS-232 management differs from the other brands ;)
>
> No two vendors wire RS-232 systems the same way.

True.

> DEC comes closer to the actual published standard than most vendors.

Yes.

> The modern PC ports using DB-9 connectors and often +/-5V signalling
> doesn't really come very close at all to the actual published standard.

It's DE9, and +-5V is within the spec. However, if you do +5/0 volts, 
you are outside.

> Making any two devices talk to one another can sometimes be an adventure,
> which is why breakout boxes are made.

Right. But if you do some thinking, a paper clip and a volt meter is all 
you need.

Typical problems are: Male vs female connectors. Ports wired as DTE or 
DCE. Using modem signalling for flow control. And different pinouts when 
using a DE9 for RS-232.

If everyone had just kept to the standard, it would have been really 
easy, but of course every company had to pervert things. As far as I 
know, DEC is/was pretty much the only one who actually followed the 
standard (although I've heard that HP actually were pretty good here as 
well).

